In article <EbKmqsK00WB8A6BWU9@andrew.cmu.edu>, co10+@andrew.cmu.edu (Carolyn Osmond) writes:
> I've recently started playing nethack on my PC.  Got the official 3.0pl9
> via ftp and every is running great.  One annoying thing has cropped
> up though.  I noticed around dungeon level 6 or 7 that things are really
> starting to slow down.  The deeper I go the slower things get.  By the way,
> my system is good ol' XT running at 4.77 with 640k.  Will this
> continue and become pretty unbearable by say...level 20?  Any thoughts?
> (no major TSR's running incidentally)   Do I have to get a 386 to get
> serious about nethack?  Thanks for any input on this.
> 
> -Brian O.
 
Nope, you shouldn't need a 386.
Most XTs these days have a 'turbo' mode running at ~ 10MHz, so use this
if you've got it.
Also, the later patch levels require a LOT of RAM to run quickly and for
PL9 about 1M is whats required. You may be able to extend your RAM by 
buying some 256 chips, but check your hardware manual about this.

When there are lots of monsters on the level etc, things do slow down a bit,
not enough to notice on a 386 (or probably even a 286), but its murder on the
system you describe.

I've got an XT 8086-1 with 640k standard and running at 4.77.
I bump it into turbo mode and I put in an extra 512k RAM and she
works fine, right down to hell. Furthest I've been is 43 (didn't quite
make Vlad's tower, but I did whip the Wiz), so I guess thats enough to
suggest that an XT is ok to get serious on.

